  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Given the Cartesian equation $9x-3y+6=0$ of the plane $\u03a0$, find an equation of $\u03a0$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-\u27e80,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,0$\n\n$\u27e9)\\cdot \u27e8$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$,0\u27e9=0$",
  "worked_solution": "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\u03a0$ is $n=\u27e89,-3,0\u27e9.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\u03a0$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =-9x-6 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,2,0)$ lies on $\u03a0.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\u27e80,2,0\u27e9.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-\u27e80,2,0\u27e9)\\cdot \u27e89,-3,0\u27e9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
